hey you might have noticed that we  didn't really talk about those coin  flips that allegedly gave victory in the  Iowa Democratic caucus to Hillary  Clinton my sense was that a lot of those  coin flip stories weren't accurate and  that indeed appears to be the case a  bunch of corporate media outlets have  been saying six consecutive coin tosses  that went in Hillary Clinton's favor  could have turned the outcome of the  entire caucus to Bernie Sanders had he  won those six consecutive coin tosses  and then people sort of disagreeing or  misunderstanding the math of how coin  tosses work the Des Moines Register  explained very clearly that these coin  flips assigned county-level delegates  they had an extremely small effect on  the overall outcome the confusion was  people believe that the coin flips were  used to determine state level delegates  now I am NOT defending the use of coin  flips in politics I i I'm not that's not  what I'm defending here I want to make  very clear is that when you talk about  county-level delegates many county-level  delegates for each of the precincts that  are involved in that Iowa caucus the  overall effect was almost none there  were a handful of democratic caucus  precincts where a delegate was awarded  with a coin toss these were up you there  they're just thousands and thousands of  these across the state from 1681  precincts not statewide delegate  equivalents but look at some of the  reporting that we saw from different  media outlets on this issue immediately  after this happened you saw reports from  for example uh and we have this to show  people NBC News political director Chuck  Todd basically we're talking a coin flip  may have decided the presidency that's  not true fox news for correspondent  shannon bream speculated that the 5 coin  tosses that five coin tosses could have  flipped the outcome fox's sean hannity  falsely claimed that the coin toss is  assigned state delegates and Drudge  Report saying America on a coin toss  also suggesting that this could have  flipped the result in Iowa that's just  not true Louis and I think people need  to understand that i  leave that Bernie Sanders is the more  progressive candidate I've been very  very clear about that but we shouldn't  now believe that the six coin tosses for  county-level delegates were going to  swing this thing in any way agreed I can  understand being outraged that coin  tosses were part of the process at all  sure and that's legitimate that's fine  but I'm not a Hillary fan at all on and  I'm not upset at this because I I  understand the scale we're talking about  absolutely but but still what we should  do away with the coin tosses there has  to be a better way I don't think we need  coin tosses in politics no doubt about  it now lastly there is confusion about  the math I saw some Hillary supporters  posting to Facebook you know the odds of  six heads in a row it's still 50 50  every time you flip a coin the odds are  fifty-fifty and then I saw Bernie  Sanders supporters saying this is  incredibly unlikely the odds that that  six coin flips in a row would go to  Hillary Clinton uh just incredibly low  let me explain the math once and for all  it is true that if you flip a coin one  time the odds of it coming up heads are  fifty-fifty assuming it's a sort of fair  coin it's been a weighted coin we  eliminate the incredibly rare  possibility that lands exactly on its  side which could happen conceivably  Louis in the physical world very very  unlikely anytime you flip a coin is  50-50 you get heads you flip it again  you still have a 50-50 chance of that  next flip coming up heads but this is a  different situation did the six coin  flip scenario is different because we're  asking about the odds of one specific  set of results six consecutive heads  results and the math is one-half or  fifty percent to the sixth power because  you're doing six of these coin flips the  coin flips one half times one half times  one half six times and that does get you  to about a one point five percent chance  of six heads flips in a row think about  it into of intuitively with roulette  roulette has 36 numbers plus zero and  double zero so there are 38 numbers the  odds that it comes up 23 on your first  spin are one in 38 let's say you hit 23  the odds that 23 comes up on the second  spin are also one in 38 but if what  you're saying before you take any spins  what are the odds  23 coming up two times in a row the odds  are 138th times 138 which is a very very  tiny number it was surprising to me  Louis the number of people who didn't  know the math on this basic probability  question uh yeah I guess so it's uh but  most people aren't having taken  statistics classes or whatever you would  take 22 you know I get you to realize  the math involved so whatever yeah I  mean even still you would think that uh  yeah anyway I guess we don't really have  to say more about it but that's the math  of it and let's understand the math  let's also understand these were  county-level delegates not state level  delegates and let's also understand that  we probably shouldn't have coin flips in  politics but let's not say that this  would have turned the Iowa caucus over  to Bernie Sanders because every  indication is that it would not have  done that i'm on twitter if you want to  comment about this at d pakman louis is  on twitter at louis MO tommy warby  parker is offering a free five day home  try-on to give you the opportunity to  check out their glasses i personally  wear Warby Parker glasses when I'm not  wearing my contact lenses and I also  started with one of these free at home  try ons of five pairs of glasses you can  get your free home try-on and support  the david pakman show at Warby Parker  trial dot-com / TDPS I don't really like  going into glasses stores and trying on  glasses well the salesperson sort of  hovers over me and I like more than 30  seconds to actually figure out whether I  like 